WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.160
When a graph contains negative edge weights, Dijkstra's greedy assumption breaks down.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:15.256
Bellman-Ford relaxes every edge in the graph across V minus one rounds, extending shortest paths by at least one hop per round.

00:00:15.256 --> 00:00:19.840
Early exit as soon as a full round produces no distance updates.

00:00:20.125 --> 00:00:27.606
If any edge can still be relaxed on the V-th round, the graph contains a reachable negative-weight cycle.

00:00:27.606 --> 00:00:35.943
To find which destinations have arbitrarily small paths, seed all nodes improved on round V into a queue and run BFS.

00:00:35.943 --> 00:00:40.645
Every node reached by that BFS can achieve negative infinity cost.

00:00:40.917 --> 00:00:50.572
For dense graphs up to four hundred vertices, Floyd-Warshall computes shortest paths between all pairs in three nested loops.

00:00:50.572 --> 00:00:59.841
The intermediate pivot vertex k must always be the outermost loop so paths build up through vertices zero to k in order.

00:00:59.841 --> 00:01:05.557
Cache the row d[i] and value d[i][k] outside the innermost loop for speed.

00:01:05.833 --> 00:01:15.064
After Floyd-Warshall finishes, any negative value on the main diagonal d[i][i] reveals a negative cycle passing through vertex i.

00:01:15.064 --> 00:01:26.084
The same cubic triple loop also solves transitive reachability and widest bottleneck paths by swapping addition and minimum for the appropriate operators.
